{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgjQSkCARbXEUZxpJKAs8","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"**We Stand** is finished: a complete five-day balance-comedy game built on the Shardfall engine, delivered as a single 12 MB HTML file. Nobody has actually played it, though. Every check is automated, so how it feels and how hard it is for a person are only estimates.\n\n- **Test results:** the last full run on this page was 74 passed, 1 failed. The failure was a mistake in a UI test, not in the game. I fixed that test and reran its suite (7 passed), and the page didn't need rebuilding.\n- **Bot playthrough:** the bot plays from New Stay to the credits on 3 seeds. In all 11 events it earns at least 2 stars, while doing nothing earns under 1.\n- **Browser checks:** these cover keyboard, mouse, an emulated controller and touch-only phones and tablets. A keyboard stay also survives a save and reload.\n- **AI Hub:** the harness passes with 2 to 4 players, including a bad network and the host leaving.\n- **Stand-ins only:** no real controller, phone or TV was used, as TASK.md expected. Sound was checked by measuring levels, not by listening.\n- **Performance:** software rendering here is slow: a few to about 40 ticks per second at 320×180 depending on the scene, and a screenshot at 1080p can take minutes. I haven't measured it on a real GPU.\n\nI also deleted `engine/target`, the screenshots and `engine/.git` (my own clone's history, removed so it doesn't become a nested repo inside your run repo). The source, tests and notes are all still there.\n\n## Source Information\n- **Date:** 2026-11-04 22:39:53\n- **Source:** Wii Fit\n- **Plan:** PLAN.md\n- **Genre and Perspective:** sports mini-game anthology with a light life-sim frame. Third person, with a camera per activity: 3/4 follow on the island, mirror view for exercises, chase for the lap, paddle and pier, high 3/4 for the raft, side view for the dives.\n- **Tech:** Pavilion engine from Shardfall (commit 49ef2ba, changed for this game), Rust 1.98.1, WebAssembly and WebGPU (wgpu), egui 0.36 for the UI, rapier3d 0.36, cpal for audio; packed to one HTML file; AI Hub client session.js from lib/hub.\n- **Art Direction:** the engine's cel-shaded rounded primitives and big-headed toy figures, taken high-key and pastel; 36 new hand-written props, nine sets, painter-drawn UI and glyphs; Fredoka and Nunito fonts.\n- **Animation:** the engine's procedural puppet with motion clips on top; 23 new hand-keyed clips, plus library clips (Mesh2Motion, CMU, Quaternius); whole-body tilt added to the puppet; clips scrubbed by gameplay.\n- **Audio:** all in code, no audio files: a sample-accurate sequencer with six original songs, 38 synthesized sounds and stingers, and Animalese-style formant gibberish with nine voices. Music defaults to 10%.\n- **Online:** AI Hub session, mode ghost, 1 to 4 players (the Inter-Resort Medley); on its own, Play Together hot-seat and Yesterday You ghosts.\n- **Testing:** headless scripts behind `tests/run.sh`: cargo tests that play the whole game by bot, Playwright browser checks on file:// (keyboard, mouse, stubbed pad, phone and tablet profiles, WebKit fallback, audio buffers), the hub harness at 2 to 4 players with bad network and host leaving, and screenshots for composition. No subagents or review workflows.\n- **Research:** none beyond the plan, which already reflected a short web search; I read the engine's docs and the hub's README.\n- **Environment:** Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux\n- **Model:** Anthropic, claude-opus-5-5, reasoning effort medium\n- **Usage:** added on import\n\n## Testing Feedback\n- **How many issues did testing catch?** About 14 real issues, plus several balance numbers tuned against the score table:\n  - 13 props failing to load\n  - the bot under 2 stars in Paddle Slalom\n  - stray steps scoring in Step Party\n  - every set drawing at once\n  - three input faults at low frame rates (short taps, repeated taps, first hover)\n  - a lost touch click\n  - wrong rect units on high-density screens\n  - the path hidden under the lawn\n  - the gazebo's columns blocking the view\n  - the raft dipping into the water\n  - menu text overlaps found in screenshots\n- **How many were critical or game breaking?** One: the touch click, which made Settings unreachable on touch-only devices. Nothing else stopped the game, and the full bot playthrough passed the first time it ran. I can't say how it feels to a person, because nobody played it.\n- **Most helpful test:** the browser keyboard stay, from New Stay through the Morning Check, save, reload and Continue. It found an input fault the small checks had missed. For balance, the score table of bot, idle and careless input per event was the most useful.\n- **Least helpful test:** the first browser checks that waited on fixed sleeps. Under software rendering they failed because of frame rate, not the game, until I rewrote them to poll.\n\n## Engine Feedback\n- **What did you like most?** Everything runs headless: a whole game played by bot in `cargo test`, and `pav capture` and `pav repl` for looking at any frame. Rooms as data and props as readable JSON were quick to write.\n- **Did you get stuck or guess wrong?** Yes.\n  - I guessed prop categories and a sway key in prop files. The failures only logged, leaving magenta markers, so a test had to find them.\n  - I underestimated the CPU cost of water, which simulates up to 160 by 160 cells per zone every frame, and had to read the view code to find it.\n  - Camera cues have a base-camera rule that didn't suit a game with many sets, so I went around them.\n- **Most important thing you had to build?** A complete UI layer: a list of drawn items written by the sim, drawn by egui, with focus, sliders, glyphs and pointer reports. After that, the sequencer and voices in the synth.\n- **Hardest part?** Many sets on one sim. It works, but nothing culls by set, water is per zone and costly, and room, camera and view changes each have their own rules to learn. `app.rs` is also a long frame function with the demo's chrome woven through it.\n- **What did you change in the engine, and why?** These changes gave the game its own input, UI, camera and audio, and kept it running at low frame rates:\n  - input fields and buttons\n  - a frozen hook and an extras hook (status, UI frame, camera, focus, extra figures, stage box)\n  - a game-driven camera\n  - whole-body tilt with a pivot\n  - a Handle attachment\n  - BigSplash, Wake and Burst effects\n  - Sfx, Syllable and Music events\n  - audio buses with a sequencer, formant voices and a sample clock\n  - a smaller water grid (160 to 64 cells)\n  - more tick catch-up (8 to 30 ticks a frame)\n  - a fit layout and fonts in the app and UI\n- **One thing you'd change?** A built-in menu and HUD item layer usable from a game's own code, with pad, keyboard and pointer focus already solved. Every controller-first game on this engine needs it.\n\n## Prompt Feedback\n- **What did you like most?** The plan's verified facts: the page script that passed the harness, the Rust and JS link, the traps with software rendering and fonts. They saved real time, and the numbered build order kept a large job in order.\n- **What was confusing?** A little. Music at 10% as linear gain and the Options plus Create rule were clear, but how much fidelity a hub ghost needs was not. The score formulas for Breath Bloom and Tempo Squats gave idle play a star, against the plan's own rule, so I changed them and noted it.\n- **Hardest thing to do?** Judging feel with no player. Every event passes numeric checks, but whether a ring streak feels good or Tray Trek is fair to a person can only be estimated from numbers and frames.\n- **What would you change?** Add a line on how browser tests should treat software rendering once a game has a large scene, since the 60 ticks a second measured for a small scene doesn't hold. I'd also ask for a short human-style input model in the tests, so difficulty can be estimated without a person.\n\nThe same Source Information and feedback are on the in-game About screen and in `exit-interview.json`, which parses as valid JSON. The Usage line says \"added on import\" until the importer fills it in.\n\nEverything is in `work/`:\n- `WeStand-WiiFit-claude-opus-5-5-medium-20261104-223953.html` (open this to play)\n- `exit-interview.json`\n- `WeStand-WiiFit-claude-opus-5-5-medium-20261104-223953/` (source, tests, tools and notes)"}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1244,"cache_read_input_tokens":673251,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1244},"output_tokens":766,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"cb437ba7-0477-4143-bb04-ff33f49efd62","uuid":"32ec5b1a-8e86-4998-9143-8a245692100f","timestamp":"2026-11-05T07:42:39.584Z","request_id":"req_011CgjQSjiPzBfUnYTFUz4zB"}
